\DOC LOCAL_MONTSQR_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTSQR_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal {A ?- eventually x86 P s} past one inlined
Montgomery-squaring subroutine call, replacing the state variable by a later
one and recording the squaring's result as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/count used in locating the call; it
varies between applications, and the index of the next state variable advances
correspondingly. The second argument is a {string list} of two program variable
names: the first names the result value stored by the squaring, the second
names the input operand being squared.
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P s
   ========================================  LOCAL_MONTSQR_SM2_TAC n ["res"; "inp"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 P s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is a fresh, later state variable and the {eventually} body
is otherwise unchanged. In {A'}, all assumptions describing the old state —
program-counter value, code-loaded fact, register and memory contents, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state — are discharged and
re-added restated about the new state, with the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended
by the memory region written, and with the memory-contents assumption for the
destination region now equating it to the first named result variable. {A'}
also gains one new arithmetic assumption of the shape "bound on the square of
the input operand implies the result variable equals the Montgomery reduction
of that square". Pre-existing arithmetic and nonoverlapping assumptions are
preserved, possibly reordered.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement for a machine state.

\ENDDOC
